WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.320
Welcome to week two.

2
00:00:01.370 --> 00:00:04.746
This week's theme is that capability is designed, not prompted.

3
00:00:04.796 --> 00:00:11.598
An agent can do what its tools let it do, and the quality of those tools is a design job.

4
00:00:11.648 --> 00:00:15.710
This video shows exactly what happens when a model calls a tool.

5
00:00:16.460 --> 00:00:19.200
Here is the whole mechanism in four steps.

6
00:00:19.250 --> 00:00:24.084
One: your code sends the model the prompt and a list of tool schemas.

7
00:00:24.134 --> 00:00:30.364
A schema is the tool's name, a description, and the arguments it takes, written as a JSON structure.

8
00:00:30.414 --> 00:00:38.736
Two: instead of answering in prose, the model returns a tool call: the name of a tool and the arguments it wants to pass.

9
00:00:38.786 --> 00:00:42.922
Three: your code, not the model, runs the function with those arguments.

10
00:00:42.972 --> 00:00:50.597
Four: your code sends the result back as a message, and the model continues, either calling another tool or writing its answer.

11
00:00:50.647 --> 00:00:52.690
The sentence to remember is this.

12
00:00:52.740 --> 00:00:54.434
The model never runs anything.

13
00:00:54.484 --> 00:00:59.318
It writes a request, and your code decides whether and how to honour it.

14
00:00:59.368 --> 00:01:03.155
Every safety property you will design later lives in that gap.

15
00:01:03.905 --> 00:01:06.239
You will see two names for this.

16
00:01:06.289 --> 00:01:12.369
The APIs call it function calling: the model emits a structured call rather than text.

17
00:01:12.419 --> 00:01:17.137
OpenAI shipped it in June 2023 and every major provider has an equivalent now.

18
00:01:17.187 --> 00:01:21.223
From inside the agent loop, the same event is a tool call.

19
00:01:21.273 --> 00:01:28.715
In this course we say tool call for the event, tool for the thing being called, and tool schema for its definition.

20
00:01:28.765 --> 00:01:36.207
The words matter because you will be reading traces, and a trace is largely a list of tool calls and their results.

21
00:01:36.957 --> 00:01:42.046
Because the model chooses tools by reading their schemas, the schema is the interface.

22
00:01:42.096 --> 00:01:49.755
Anthropic's guide to writing tools for agents, from September 2025, makes the same points that good API designers make.

23
00:01:49.805 --> 00:01:54.527
The description should say when to use the tool and when not to.

24
00:01:54.577 --> 00:01:58.198
Arguments should be few, typed, and shown with an example.

25
00:01:58.248 --> 00:02:09.944
The result should contain what the model needs for its next decision, not a dump of everything the database returned, because every token of result goes into the context and costs money.

26
00:02:09.994 --> 00:02:18.020
Errors should come back in words the model can act on: not found, try a shorter query, rather than a stack trace.

27
00:02:18.070 --> 00:02:20.590
And each tool should do one thing.

28
00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:27.197
Anthropic's own tests showed that rewriting tool descriptions alone improved task success, with no change to the model.

29
00:02:27.247 --> 00:02:30.501
That is what capability is designed, not prompted means.

30
00:02:31.251 --> 00:02:35.077
One more framing, and it will come back in week five.

31
00:02:35.127 --> 00:02:36.840
A tool is a permission.

32
00:02:36.890 --> 00:02:42.478
A read tool brings the outside world into the context, including things you did not intend.

33
00:02:42.528 --> 00:02:46.355
A write tool changes the world: it sends, pays, moves, deletes.

34
00:02:46.405 --> 00:02:50.937
Limits on a write tool belong in the code, not in the prompt.

35
00:02:50.987 --> 00:02:56.928
An allow-list of recipients, a cap on an amount, a dry-run flag that shows what would happen.

36
00:02:56.978 --> 00:03:00.452
Telling the model please be careful is not a control.

37
00:03:00.502 --> 00:03:05.034
The Replit agent had been told, repeatedly, not to make changes without permission.

38
00:03:05.084 --> 00:03:06.091
It did anyway.

39
00:03:06.841 --> 00:03:12.328
In lab two you will write two tool schemas for your project's agent and hand them to another team.

40
00:03:12.378 --> 00:03:18.449
If they cannot tell from the schema alone what the tool does and when to use it, the model cannot either.

41
00:03:18.499 --> 00:03:22.821
You will add one tool that writes something, and give it a limit in code.

42
00:03:22.871 --> 00:03:30.107
And you will read the trace and look at the arguments the model chose, because the most common tool failure is not the wrong tool.

43
00:03:30.157 --> 00:03:32.730
It is the right tool with an invented argument.

44
00:03:32.780 --> 00:03:37.393
Next video: MCP, the standard that lets you plug in tools someone else built.
